xilinx-vip: accept the ADV7611 RGB888 media bus code

The HDMI input on the RK-ZYNQ7100 is an ADV7611, driven by the mainline
adv7604 driver, and its 24-bit RGB source pad reports
MEDIA_BUS_FMT_RGB888_1X24. The Xilinx VIP format table only knows
MEDIA_BUS_FMT_RBG888_1X24, so with the two joined by xilinx-vipp:

  xvip_get_format_by_code(RGB888_1X24)  -> -EINVAL
  VIDIOC_ENUM_FMT / VIDIOC_G_FMT        -> -EINVAL
  VIDIOC_STREAMON                       -> -EINVAL

xvip_dma_verify_format() compares dma->fmtinfo->code, which comes from
xvip_get_format_by_fourcc(), against the code the subdev reports, so the
new entry has to come BEFORE the existing RBG888/BGR24 one: both scans
return their first match, and only this order makes the two agree.

The byte order is not a coincidence. The ADV7611 in SDR 4:4:4 8-bit with
OP_CH_SEL = RGB drives P23-P16 = R, P15-P8 = G, P7-P0 = B, which the
capture block passes straight through, and the VDMA writes TDATA[7:0]
to the first byte in memory. So memory reads B, G, R -- which is what
V4L2_PIX_FMT_BGR24 means.

Nothing that was resolvable before resolves differently: the RBG888
entries are untouched, and by_fourcc(RGB24) still finds the RBG one.

Signed-off-by: Howard M. Harte <hharte@magicandroidapps.com>

--- a/drivers/media/platform/xilinx/xilinx-vip.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/xilinx/xilinx-vip.c
@@ -80,6 +80,8 @@
 	  2, 24, V4L2_PIX_FMT_X416, 1, 1, 1, 1 },
 	{ XVIP_VF_YUV_444, 16, NULL, MEDIA_BUS_FMT_VUY16_1X48,
 	  2, 24, V4L2_PIX_FMT_X416M, 1, 1, 1, 1 },
+	{ XVIP_VF_RBG, 8, NULL, MEDIA_BUS_FMT_RGB888_1X24,
+	  3, 24, V4L2_PIX_FMT_BGR24, 1, 1, 1, 1 },
 	{ XVIP_VF_RBG, 8, NULL, MEDIA_BUS_FMT_RBG888_1X24,
 	  3, 24, V4L2_PIX_FMT_BGR24, 1, 1, 1, 1 },
 	{ XVIP_VF_RBG, 8, NULL, MEDIA_BUS_FMT_RBG888_1X24,
